@namespace Ben.Web.Website.Library.Kit
@using Ben.Web.Services.WebApi
@inject NavigationManager Nav
@typeparam TItem

@*
    The four states one thing actually has: loading, signed out, could not load, and here it is.

    BenListState did this for lists (item 120); nothing did it for a single object, because
    GetAsync answered a 401, a 403, a 404 and an empty success with the same null and there was
    nothing to render a difference from. ItemResult carries the difference now, and the hosted
    events plan of record (2026-09-12) made "a 403 must not read as not-found" a rule for every
    screen it builds — a member refused the dietary sheet is not looking at an event with no
    guests. This is the component that keeps that rule without each page re-deriving it.

    The same four branches as BenListState, in the same order, with the same words, on purpose: a
    person who has learned what the yellow card means on a list should not have to learn it again
    because the page shows one record. There is no empty branch — a successful fetch that carries
    nothing (a 204) renders nothing, because "nothing here yet" is a list's sentence and a single
    object has no such state worth a card.

    ChildContent is typed. The page writes @context.Title, not _record!.Title, and cannot reach the
    content branch with a null: that is the whole point of a result type, and a component that
    handed back a nullable would have thrown the type's guarantee away at the last step.
*@

@if (Loading)
{
    <BenLoaderOverlay Visible="true" Text="@(LoadingText ?? "Loading…")" />
}
else if (Result.SessionExpired)
{
    @* 401 is the one failure that is not about this record at all — see BenListState for the
       history. The link carries the current page so signing in returns here, and it is an
       anchor, which works whether or not the circuit that just died is replaced. *@
    <div class="card border-warning">
        <div class="card-body text-center py-4">
            <h6 class="mb-2">You've been signed out</h6>
            <p class="text-secondary small mb-3">
                Your session ended, so this couldn't be loaded. Signing in again will bring you
                straight back to this page.
            </p>
            <a href="@SignInUrl" class="btn btn-primary btn-sm">Sign in again</a>
        </div>
    </div>
}
else if (Result.Failed)
{
    @* Says the load failed, not "you do not have permission": a 500 and a dropped connection
       reach here identically, and guessing at the cause is how a page tells its second untruth.
       When the server gave a sentence worth reading, that sentence is shown instead. *@
    <div class="card border-warning">
        <div class="card-body text-center py-4">
            <h6 class="mb-2">Couldn't load this</h6>
            <p class="text-secondary small mb-3">
                @(Result.Reason ?? "Something went wrong fetching this. It is still there — "
                                 + "this is a problem reaching the server, not a missing record.")
            </p>
            @if (OnRetry.HasDelegate)
            {
                <button type="button" class="btn btn-outline-secondary btn-sm" @onclick="OnRetry">
                    Try again
                </button>
            }
        </div>
    </div>
}
else if (Result.Item is { } item && ChildContent is not null)
{
    @ChildContent(item)
}

@code {
    /// <summary>The fetch's outcome. Default (not failed, no item) renders nothing.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public ItemResult<TItem> Result { get; set; }

    /// <summary>True while the fetch is in flight — takes precedence over everything below.</summary>
    [Parameter] public bool Loading { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Overrides the spinner's caption.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string? LoadingText { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Rendered with the record when there is one. Typed, so the page never dereferences a maybe.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public RenderFragment<TItem>? ChildContent { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Wire this and the failure state offers a Try again button.</summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Not offered for <see cref="ItemResult{T}.SessionExpired"/>: a retry on a dead session is a
    /// button that cannot work.
    /// </remarks>
    [Parameter] public EventCallback OnRetry { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Sign-in, carrying this page as the place to return to.</summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Relative, because <c>Login.razor</c> refuses a ReturnUrl that does not start with a single
    /// slash — its open-redirect guard, which an absolute URI would trip.
    /// </remarks>
    private string SignInUrl
    {
        get
        {
            var here = Nav.ToBaseRelativePath(Nav.Uri);
            return $"/login?returnUrl={Uri.EscapeDataString("/" + here)}";
        }
    }
}
